@5014 = ~Durlag's Letter to Kiel~

@5015 = ~Kiel, my firstborn, my brightest gem. Forgive me.

My blindness and festering paranoia allowed the beasts of a hundred faces to steal your life while I saw enemies in my own shadows. Rest now in the peace that will forever elude your father. I have cursed these very walls and bound the deep earth so your sleep remains undisturbed. I have steeped your flesh in our clan's ancient runic wards and embalmed you with the rarest essences of the Underdark—Time itself shall not touch your brow so long as this tower stands. You shall remain whole, looking as though you merely closed your eyes, waiting for a dawn that will never come for us.

At your feet, I lay your morning star and buckler. Guarding your rest are my final, flawless creations—the Dwarven Doom Guards. I forged these iron shells myself and breathed into them a tireless, unyielding wrath. They know no fear, they feel no fatigue, and they possess no living hearts for the shapeshifters to deceive. Should any intruder, living or dead, dare reach for your birthright... my iron hounds will tear them asunder.

Sleep, my boy. Your father keeps watch.
— Durlag~

@5016 = ~Mechezarin~

@5017 = ~Mechezarin.

Of all the creatures I dragged down into the darkness of my menagerie, he was the greatest—and my heaviest burden. He was barely out of his hatchling years when my clan snared him in the Earthspur Mountains, dragging him into these cavernous depths. I built this massive vault just to hold him, a grand trophy to make my guests marvel at the might of the Trollkiller.

But a dragon is not meant for the cradle of stone. Over the decades, the damp cavern air and the endless, maddening ring of dwarven hammers broke his body. His magnificent wings, meant to catch the winds of the Trackless Sea, withered and never grew to their true span, scraping uselessly against the cold cave floor. His horns grew twisted, stunted by the lack of the open sky. I brought in the mage Daitel to mend him, but magic cannot replace the sun; all the wizard could do was reinforce the beast's tail, fusing bone to steel to help him bear his own colossal, unnatural weight.

Yet, in that shared prison, a strange thing grew between us. Trust. Or perhaps just a mutual understanding of isolation. Mechezarin hated his captivity—I felt his burning fury every time his blind, pale eyes followed me through the gloom—but he also knew that I was the only soul who truly looked at him without fear. He ate from my hand, and in his low, rumbling growls, I fancied he spoke to me. He became the only creature in this tower whose hatred was honest. Unlike the shape-shifting maggots that later stole my family's skin, Mechezarin never wore a mask.~

